  "response": "Where an operator and a fabricator advance incompatible accounts of a vessel failure, engineers resolve the dispute by reconstructing the event from physical and process evidence rather than by weighing assertions against one another. The vessel ruptured while in service at the operator's plant, and the violence of that rupture killed two workers. Either that fatal outcome is what occurred or no workers were injured in the rupture; one of the two must be the case, and they cannot both be. The operator attributed the rupture to a welding defect introduced during fabrication, whereas the fabricator attributed the rupture to operation above the vessel's design pressure.\n\nNeither account is decisive on its own, so the inquiry turns first to evidence that could only be gathered after the vessel had burst. Investigators recovered fragments of the ruptured vessel shell for metallurgical examination; as a result, chevron markings on the fracture surface were traced back to locate the fracture origin. Scanning electron microscopy of the origin region revealed cleavage fracture features.\n\nTwo general propositions about laboratory practice govern how a surface of this kind is read, and they must be stated before any conclusion is drawn from it. Cleavage fracture surfaces display striations whose spacing records the number of pressure cycles endured. Charpy V-notch testing measures the rate of fatigue crack growth in weld metal, a proposition that cannot be squared with the fracture morphology recorded at the initiation site; the two readings of that surface cannot both stand.\n\nThe plant's distributed control system archive supplied the pressure and temperature history for the hours preceding the rupture, and that record led to finite element analysis being used to estimate the stress state at the fracture initiation site. The computed stresses are then compared with the vessel's design basis and with the as-built quality of its seams. On the strength of the full record, at least one of two conditions obtained, and perhaps both: the longitudinal seam weld contained a lack-of-fusion flaw created during fabrication, and the vessel was operated at a pressure above its maximum allowable working pressure. The hour-by-hour trace retrieved from the control system archive is what establishes the second of these conditions.\n\nOnly once the initiation site, the fracture mode and the service history have been fixed in this way can responsibility be apportioned between the shop and the plant. Where a fabrication flaw and an overpressure excursion coexist, the engineering question becomes which of them would have been sufficient alone, and that question is settled by comparing the critical flaw size calculated for the measured stress with the dimensions of the flaw actually present, not by the narrative either party prefers.",
